---
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { handoverSource, handoverValidate } from '../data/examples';
import { CORE_REPO, LICENSE_NAME, PYPI_URL, milestone, specs, versions } from '../data/facts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="RoboLang: a common language for robotic tasks"
  description="RoboLang is an open-source, robot-independent language for robotic tasks. Declare a goal, validate the task, and run it in a deterministic symbolic simulation that succeeds only when the goal is verified. Not for real hardware."
>
  <section slot="head" class="hero on-ink">
    <div class="wrap">
      <ul class="chips" aria-label="Project facts">
        <li><span class="chip"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true"></span>Developer preview</span></li>
        <li><span class="chip">Open source</span></li>
        <li><span class="chip">Language {versions.language}</span></li>
      </ul>
      <h1>A common language for robotic tasks.</h1>
      <div class="signal-rules" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <div class="hero-grid">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <p class="lede">
            Write, validate and simulate structured robot tasks. Built for developers and AI systems.
            No hardware required.
          </p>
          <p>
            RoboLang describes <em>what</em> a robot should do in a small, typed, human-readable text
            format: what must be true before it starts, the <strong>goal</strong> it must achieve, the
            steps and the safety limits. Its tools check a task file, show exactly what it says and run
            it in a deterministic symbolic simulation. A run succeeds only when the goal is verified in
            the final world, not merely because the steps ran. An experimental Core {versions.core}
            carries that goal, in simulation only, from an AI planner to a robot and back as a verified
            outcome.
          </p>
          <div class="actions">
            <a class="button" href="/get-started/">Get started</a>
            <a class="button secondary" href={CORE_REPO}>View on GitHub</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="cropframe">
          <CodeBlock lang="rob" code={handoverSource} label="examples/handover.rob" />
          <CodeBlock code={handoverValidate} label="$ robolang validate examples/handover.rob" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <dl class="specstrip">
        <div><dt>Distribution</dt><dd>{versions.distribution}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Language</dt><dd>{versions.language}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>IR schema</dt><dd>{versions.irSchema}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Python</dt><dd>{versions.pythonMinimum}+</dd></div>
        <div><dt>License</dt><dd>{LICENSE_NAME}</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <div class="section-body prose">
      <p>
        Robot tasks are often written as ad hoc scripts or free-form text. A typo, a missing argument
        or a name that was never declared is found late, usually when something is already running.
        That gets worse when the task text is produced by a language model instead of a person.
      </p>
      <p>
        RoboLang's purpose is to give a task a precise written form with a published grammar, so that
        tools can reject a malformed task with a clear message and hand a well-formed one to other
        software as structured data.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>What works today</h2>
    <div class="section-body">
      <p class="prose">
        Distribution {versions.distribution} is on <a href={PYPI_URL}>PyPI</a>:
        <code>pip install robolang</code> installs one command, <code>robolang</code>. It needs Python
        {versions.pythonMinimum} or newer and has no other dependencies. Nothing it does reaches a
        robot.
      </p>
      <h3>Available</h3>
      <ul class="cards">
        <li class="card">
          <p class="syslabel">A / Language</p>
          <h3>Write</h3>
          <p>
            Languages {versions.language} have a formal grammar: typed parameters, <code>@safety</code>
            annotations, a <code>pre</code> block, a <code>goal</code> block (1.3) and a
            <code>plan</code>. 1.2 adds <code>and</code>, <code>or</code> and <code>not</code>; 1.4
            adds bounded <code>if</code> / <code>else</code> and <code>fail</code>.
            <code>robolang reference</code> prints the language reference.
          </p>
          <p><a href="/reference/">Language reference</a></p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <p class="syslabel">B / Validator</p>
          <h3>Validate</h3>
          <p>
            <code>robolang validate</code> checks syntax, names, types and values, and reports every
            problem as <code>file:line:column</code> with a stable diagnostic code. With
            <code>--world</code> it also reports failures a run would certainly hit in that world.
          </p>
          <p><a href="/examples/#invalid">See diagnostics</a></p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <p class="syslabel">C / IR</p>
          <h3>Inspect</h3>
          <p>
            <code>robolang inspect</code> shows what a valid file describes, as text or as JSON in IR
            schema {versions.irSchema}, for use by other tools. Every command also reads Task IR back
            with <code>--ir</code> and re-validates it from scratch.
          </p>
          <p><a href="/examples/#json-ir">See the JSON IR</a></p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <p class="syslabel">D / Simulation</p>
          <h3>Simulate</h3>
          <p>
            <code>robolang simulate</code> runs a validated task against a declared world file. It
            fails closed on a false or unknown precondition, and succeeds only when every goal
            condition is true in the final world. The same inputs always give the same run record.
          </p>
          <p><a href="/examples/#goals">See a goal checked</a></p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Experimental, simulation only</h3>
      <ul class="cards">
        <li class="card">
          <p class="syslabel">E / Core {versions.core}</p>
          <h3>Delegate</h3>
          <p>
            <code>robolang delegate</code> sends a task's goal, never its plan, as a signed Core
            {versions.core} message. The robot runs a plan from its own <code>.rob</code> library and
            reports an outcome, and the planner accepts success only after re-checking the evidence.
            <code>export-goal</code> prints the goal message on its own.
          </p>
          <p><a href="/examples/#delegate">See a delegation</a></p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <p class="syslabel">F / Core {versions.core}</p>
          <h3>Exchange</h3>
          <p>
            Core {versions.core} defines checked messages between AI agents and robots (A2R) and
            between robots (R2R). <code>robolang arch02</code> runs the reference scenario in one
            process: stub participants, local authorization, and success only with evidence. Gate 1
            of RL-ARCH-02 passed on {milestone.date}.
          </p>
          <p><a href="/examples/#arch02">See the reference simulation</a></p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Future</h3>
      <p class="prose">
        Real transports and network interoperability, independently built implementations,
        physical-robot execution and richer world models. None of these is in the package. Work in
        the core repository since this release, including language 1.5, Core 0.2 and a ROS 2 backend
        in a physics simulator, is on the
        <a href="/roadmap/">Vision &amp; Roadmap</a> page shows the status of each stage.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 id="limitations">What it does not do</h2>
    <div class="section-body">
      <p class="prose">
        These limits are deliberate for the developer preview. Please read them before you start.
      </p>
      <dl class="readout">
        <div>
          <dt>Robot execution <span class="status status--off">Not available</span></dt>
          <dd>
            <strong>It does not run tasks on robots.</strong> Nothing in the supported package moves a
            robot. <code>simulate</code> and <code>arch02</code> track symbolic facts only: no motion
            planning, kinematics, collisions or physics.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Safety enforcement <span class="status status--off">Not available</span></dt>
          <dd>
            <strong>It does not enforce safety.</strong>
            <code>@safety</code> limits are checked against the simulated robot profile and reported in
            the run record, never enforced. <code>pre</code> conditions are evaluated only against the
            symbolic world file. A file that validates, or a run that succeeds in simulation, is not
            thereby safe to run on hardware.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Interoperability <span class="status status--on">Experimental</span></dt>
          <dd>
            <strong>It is not a robot protocol.</strong> Core {versions.core} is a draft, exercised only
            in one process with simulation-only signatures. There is no network transport and no
            independent implementation yet. A valid Core message is never permission to operate a
            robot. See the <a href={specs.core}>Core {versions.core} specification</a>.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>ROS 2 <span class="status status--off">Not supported</span></dt>
          <dd>
            <strong>The installed package has no ROS 2 integration.</strong> A separate, experimental
            ROS 2 backend runs <code>.rob</code> tasks in the Gazebo physics simulator in the core
            repository's CI; it is not part of <code>pip install robolang</code> and never drives
            hardware. See the <a href="/roadmap/#status">roadmap</a>. The early v1 ROS 2 adapter is
            kept as historical source, never validated and not supported.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>AI generation <span class="status status--off">Not included</span></dt>
          <dd>
            <strong>It does not generate tasks with AI.</strong> The language is designed to be easy
            for language models to write, and the core repository benchmarks how well models write
            it, but no model is included. The planners in <code>delegate</code> and
            <code>arch02</code> are scripted stubs.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Release <span class="status status--on">Alpha</span></dt>
          <dd>
            <strong>It is an alpha.</strong> Version {versions.distribution} is published on PyPI.
            Details may change between versions.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p class="prose">
        Robot execution, networked interoperability and AI-assisted authoring are research
        directions, described on the <a href="/roadmap/">Vision &amp; Roadmap</a> page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Who it is for</h2>
    <div class="section-body prose">
      <p>
        Robotics developers, ROS 2 engineers and researchers who want to experiment with a checkable
        task format, including as a target for tasks written by language models. Feedback on the
        language and the tools is the main thing the project needs right now.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button" href="/get-started/">Install and try it</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href="/contribute/">Give feedback</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
